{%- comment -%}
  PG Cart Sticky: keep the cart reachable while the product page is browsed.

  THE PROBLEM, MEASURED. The product page (#pgx) is 7433px tall on a 390px
  phone -- about nine screens -- and 4029px on desktop. Its header, #pgx
  .pgx-head, is position:relative, so it and the only cart control on the page
  (a.pgx-cart, a bare SVG in .pgx-ic) scroll away for good after roughly the
  first 180px. Measured at scrollY 2200 the cart icon sits at y=-2116 on mobile
  and y=-2067 on desktop: off screen, unreachable. A shopper who adds an orb and
  keeps browsing has no way back to the cart short of scrolling to the very top.

  WHAT THIS ADDS. A full-width bar pinned across the top, sliding in once the
  page's own cart control has scrolled slightly out of view so it never sits on
  top of the wordmark or the announcement carousel. It carries the
  live item count
  and, while the 10-minute offer window is open, the countdown and the 10% OFF
  label -- so the reason to check out travels with the shopper down a nine-screen
  page instead of living only inside the cart. Tapping anywhere on it opens the
  cart the same way the header icon does.

  IT IS A BAR, NOT A BUBBLE. This began as a 46px round button in the corner and
  the empty strip beside it was dead space; the bar uses that width for the
  countdown, which is the part that actually drives the checkout.

  WHY THE TOP AND NOT THE USUAL BOTTOM. The bottom of the product page is already
  taken: #pg-sticky (z-index 55) is a fixed full-width bar 87px tall carrying the
  price and ADD TO CART, visible for the whole scroll. A bottom bar would land on
  top of it. The top is free, and it is where the cart already lives in the
  header, so it matches the shopper's muscle memory.

  WHY IT CLICKS THE HEADER ICON INSTEAD OF OPENING THE DRAWER ITSELF. The theme
  binds cart opening through document-delegated handlers, and pg-no-cart-page
  intercepts /cart navigation. Rather than duplicate any of that -- and risk
  drifting from it later -- this forwards to the real a.pgx-cart element and lets
  the theme do what it already does, falling back to a plain /cart navigation.

  THE COUNT comes from /cart.js, because a.pgx-cart is only an SVG with no badge
  to mirror. Counting <li> rows in the drawer was rejected -- that counts LINES,
  not quantities, so one line of quantity 3 would read as 1. The COUNTDOWN is
  read from window.pgRush (pg-cart-timer) rather than kept here, so the bar and
  the cart can never show two different times.

  z-index 90: above the page and above #pg-sticky's 55, below the cart drawer's
  160 so an open drawer covers it, and far below the cookie/consent banners.

  IT WAITS FOR A SMALL SCROLL, AND ONLY A SMALL ONE. For a while it was on from
  the first paint, and on a phone that was worse than the problem it solved: the
  bar sat on top of the POCKET ERA wordmark and the announcement carousel, the
  two things a shopper sees first. It now slides in the moment the page's own
  cart control clears the top edge -- not a screen later, six pixels later --
  so the top of the page is clean and the bar is there for the whole of the
  remaining eight screens, on phones and on desktop alike.

  AND IT NO LONGER HIDES ON AN EMPTY CART. Hiding it there was the same mistake
  one step earlier: the shopper with nothing in the cart is exactly the one the
  offer is aimed at, and they were the only one who never saw it. With an empty
  cart the bar reads ADD TO CART FOR EXTRA 10% OFF and runs its own ten-minute
  clock so the offer is visible before there is anything to discount.

  THAT CLOCK IS A SEPARATE, HONEST ONE. pg-cart-timer's window only exists once
  there is a cart -- it clears its deadline when the cart empties -- so this
  keeps its own deadline under pgRushPeek. It is persisted, so it does not
  restart on every page view, and it is cleared as soon as something is added,
  because from that point pg-cart-timer's real window is the one that counts (an
  add opens a fresh ten minutes, so nobody is ever shown less time than they
  get). When it runs out the clock simply goes away and the bar keeps offering
  the deal, rather than resetting to 10:00 and pretending the urgency renewed.

  THE WORDING IS MEASURED, AND THE LONG FORM IS OPT-IN. "ADD TO CART FOR EXTRA
  10% OFF" wants 265px beside a clock and a bag; "ADD FOR EXTRA 10% OFF" wants
  165px. The message box is 183px on a 320px phone and 239px inside the capped
  380px desktop bar, so the long sentence fits in neither. Hiding it below 431px
  was not enough: at 431px the phone block stops applying and the copy, the two
  font sizes, both gaps, the bar padding and the bag icon all grow in one step,
  leaving 0.1px of slack -- a clip in real Poppins -- and above 900px the bar is
  capped to 380px while the font returns to 14.5px, which silently ate the word
  OFF on every desktop with an empty cart. So the long form is now shown only
  across 460-899px, where the smallest measured margin is +29px, and hidden
  everywhere else. The item wording ("1 ITEM . EXTRA 10% OFF") always fitted and
  is untouched.

  AND IT IS CENTRED. The clock and the offer are one sentence and are centred
  together in the space the bag icon leaves, with `justify-content:safe center`.
  They used to be pushed to opposite ends by an auto margin on the offer, which
  put 94px of dead black between them on a 390px phone and 344px on a tablet.
  Centring costs no width -- justify-content only distributes space left over
  after flex sizing -- so the 320px slack above is unchanged by it.

  Hidden while the drawer is open. Gated on #pgx so it appears on product pages
  only, as asked.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  #pg-cartfab{
    position:fixed;
    top:max(10px, env(safe-area-inset-top));
    left:12px;right:12px;
    z-index:90;
    height:54px;
    display:flex;align-items:center;gap:11px;
    padding:0 9px 0 18px;
    border-radius:27px;
    background:rgba(11,7,16,.94);
    border:1px solid rgba(190,140,235,.75);
    /* purple glow: a lift shadow plus two coloured halos, gently pulsing so the
       bar reads as live without strobing. Motion is dropped for anyone who asks
       for reduced motion; the glow itself stays. */
    box-shadow:0 10px 28px -10px rgba(0,0,0,.8),
               0 0 22px rgba(139,68,190,.75),
               0 0 54px rgba(139,68,190,.38);
    animation:pgCsGlow 2.6s ease-in-out infinite;
    color:#F1EDF7;
    -webkit-tap-highlight-color:transparent;
    cursor:pointer;
    opacity:0;
    transform:translateY(-8px);
    pointer-events:none;
    transition:opacity .18s ease, transform .18s ease;
  }
  #pg-cartfab.pg-cs-on{opacity:1;transform:none;pointer-events:auto}
  @keyframes pgCsGlow{
    0%,100%{box-shadow:0 10px 28px -10px rgba(0,0,0,.8),
                       0 0 18px rgba(139,68,190,.60),
                       0 0 44px rgba(139,68,190,.28)}
    50%    {box-shadow:0 10px 28px -10px rgba(0,0,0,.8),
                       0 0 30px rgba(139,68,190,.95),
                       0 0 72px rgba(139,68,190,.50)}
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
    #pg-cartfab{animation:none}
  }

  /* the message fills the width the old round button wasted */
  /* NO TRAILING "...". The line was `overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis`, so
     the moment the copy outran the bar -- which it does on a 390px phone once
     the clock and the 40px bag have taken their share -- it cut the sentence off
     mid-word and left an ellipsis where the offer should be. The wording is
     shortened at the source instead (see paint), and the letter-spacing eased,
     so there is nothing to truncate; overflow is clipped rather than
     ellipsised so a stray pixel cannot bring the dots back. */
  #pg-cartfab .pg-cs-msg{
    flex:1 1 auto;min-width:0;
    display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;
    /* CENTRED AS ONE GROUP, WITH A FALLBACK, AND THE FALLBACK IS ONLY SAFE
       BECAUSE OF AN INVARIANT -- read this before changing the children.

       This row is white-space:nowrap with overflow:hidden. A plain `center` on
       a flex row that OVERFLOWS splits the spill across both edges, and the
       start-side half is unreachable -- overflow:hidden eats it with no scroll
       origin. Measured in that state: the clock renders at x=-111px, entirely
       off the bar, while the tail of the offer is cut at the same time. Both
       ends lost. `safe center` exists precisely for that: it reverts to start
       the moment the content would overflow.

       THE INVARIANT: this row cannot overflow. Its only rigid child is the
       clock (flex:0 0 auto, 42-50px); the offer is flex:0 1 auto with
       min-width:0 and shrinks instead. The narrowest box the row ever gets is
       183px at 320px wide, so the rigid content is never more than a third of
       it. That is why the bare `center` fallback below is safe here even though
       it would not be in general -- it is only ever consulted by engines too
       old to parse `safe` (iOS Safari before 16), and on those engines the
       overflow state it mishandles does not arise. Without the fallback those
       devices would drop the declaration entirely, get `normal` (= flex-start),
       and still show the off-centre bar this change exists to fix.

       If you ever add a child here that cannot shrink -- a second pill, an
       icon, a flex:0 0 auto on the offer -- delete the bare `center` line. */
    justify-content:center;
    justify-content:safe center;
    font-size:14.5px;font-weight:800;line-height:1.15;
    text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.03em;
    white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:clip;
  }
  #pg-cartfab .pg-cs-clock{
    font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;font-weight:900;
    font-size:15.5px;letter-spacing:.02em;
    background:#8B44BE;color:#fff;
    padding:4px 9px;border-radius:7px;
    box-shadow:0 0 12px rgba(139,68,190,.7);
    flex:0 0 auto;
  }
  /* .pg-cs-msg is flex:1 1 auto, so it takes the whole width the clock and the
     bag leave over. This used to carry `margin-left:auto`, which right-packed
     the offer against the bag icon: an auto margin absorbs ALL free space before
     justify-content is even consulted, so every spare pixel landed in one lump
     between the timer and the offer. Measured gap left-of-text vs right-of-text:
     94/9px at 390px, 344/11px at 768px -- it read as a layout failure rather than
     a design, and shortening the phone copy in the previous change widened the
     void by another 69px. The clock and the offer are one sentence, so they are
     centred together by .pg-cs-msg above and the auto margin is gone. Centring
     costs no width: justify-content only distributes space left over AFTER flex
     sizing, so the measured slack at 320px is byte-identical either way. */
  #pg-cartfab .pg-cs-sub{
    min-width:0;
    color:#EFE6FA;font-weight:800;overflow:hidden;
    /* clip, NOT ellipsis. The parent was already switched to clip so a tight
       fit could not put dots back on the end of the offer; leaving ellipsis on
       the child meant the dots came back the moment the child was the thing
       that overflowed, which is exactly what happened on a 360px phone. */
    text-overflow:clip;
  }
  #pg-cartfab .pg-cs-sub b{color:#D9B4FF;font-weight:900}

  /* " TO CART" IS OPT-IN, NOT OPT-OUT. It used to be hidden only inside
     @media(max-width:430px), i.e. shown everywhere else -- and everywhere else
     included two bands where it does not fit:
       431-459px  the phone block stops applying, so the copy jumps back to the
                  long form AND the fonts/paddings/icon all grow in the same
                  step. Measured slack at 431px: +0.1px. Real Poppins is wider
                  than the fallback this was measured in, so that is a clip.
       >=900px    the bar is capped to 380px (see the note further down) while
                  the font is back to 14.5px, so the sentence wants 265.6px in
                  238.7px and silently loses the word OFF -- on a desktop with an
                  empty cart, which is the default first-visit state. It read
                  "ADD TO CART FOR EXTRA 10%".
     So it is hidden by default and shown only across 460-899px, where the
     smallest measured margin is +29px. Nudging the 430px breakpoint instead
     would drag the phone font/padding/icon sizes up with it, which is a
     different concern and a different look. */
  #pg-cartfab .pg-cs-long{display:none}
  @media (min-width:460px) and (max-width:899px){
    #pg-cartfab .pg-cs-long{display:inline}
  }

  /* the icon keeps its old corner position at the right-hand end */
  #pg-cartfab .pg-cs-ic{
    position:relative;flex:0 0 auto;
    width:44px;height:44px;border-radius:50%;
    display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;
    background:rgba(168,120,214,.22);
    box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(190,140,235,.35);
  }
  #pg-cartfab svg{width:26px;height:26px;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.9;
    stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
  #pg-cartfab .pg-cs-n{
    position:absolute;top:-2px;right:-2px;
    min-width:21px;height:21px;padding:0 6px;
    display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;
    border-radius:9px;
    background:#8B44BE;color:#fff;
    font-size:12px;font-weight:900;line-height:1;
    box-shadow:0 0 0 2px rgba(11,7,16,.94), 0 0 10px rgba(139,68,190,.8);
  }
  #pg-cartfab:active{transform:scale(.99)}
  html.m6pn-open #pg-cartfab{opacity:0 !important;pointer-events:none !important}

  /* the copy is longer than it was, so tighten it on narrow phones rather than
     let it ellipsis away the words that carry the offer */
  @media (max-width:430px){
    /* MEASURED. "ADD TO CART FOR EXTRA 10% OFF" wants 225px. On a 320px phone
       the message box is 175px and on a 360px phone 215px, so it was cut off on
       both; at 390px it fitted in exactly 225px, i.e. with zero slack, which
       real Poppins (wider than the headless fallback) turns into a clip too.
       The wording drops to "ADD FOR EXTRA 10% OFF" here -- 21 characters
       instead of 29, about 165px -- which clears 320px with 18px to spare. The
       item wording ("1 ITEM . EXTRA 10% OFF") already fitted everywhere and is
       untouched. */
    /* and 4px of the bar's own inset back on each side, which is 8px of message */
    #pg-cartfab{left:8px;right:8px}
    #pg-cartfab{padding:0 8px 0 14px;gap:9px}
    #pg-cartfab .pg-cs-msg{font-size:12.5px;letter-spacing:.02em;gap:6px}
    #pg-cartfab .pg-cs-clock{font-size:13.5px;padding:3px 7px}
    #pg-cartfab .pg-cs-ic{width:40px;height:40px}
    #pg-cartfab svg{width:23px;height:23px}
  }

  @media (min-width:900px){
    /* a full-width bar across a desktop page reads as a site banner, so cap it
       and keep it in the corner the cart already occupies */
    #pg-cartfab{left:auto;width:380px;max-width:calc(100vw - 24px)}
  }
</style>
<script>
(function(){
  if (window.pgCartSticky) return; window.pgCartSticky = 1;

  var fab, badge, msg, count = 0;

  function headerCart(){ return document.querySelector('#pgx a.pgx-cart, a.pgx-cart'); }
  function rush(){ return window.pgRush && window.pgRush.active() ? window.pgRush : null; }

  /* the empty-cart preview window -- see the note above */
  var PKEY = 'pgRushPeek', PEEK_MS = 10 * 60 * 1000;
  function peekLeft(){
    var end = 0;
    try { end = parseInt(localStorage.getItem(PKEY), 10) || 0; } catch(e){}
    if (!end){
      end = Date.now() + PEEK_MS;
      try { localStorage.setItem(PKEY, String(end)); } catch(e){}
    }
    return Math.max(0, end - Date.now());
  }
  function peekClear(){ try { localStorage.removeItem(PKEY); } catch(e){} }
  function clockMs(ms){
    var s = Math.ceil(ms / 1000), m = Math.floor(s / 60), r = s % 60;
    return m + ':' + (r < 10 ? '0' : '') + r;
  }

  function build(){
    if (fab) return fab;
    fab = document.createElement('div');
    fab.id = 'pg-cartfab';
    fab.setAttribute('role', 'button');
    fab.setAttribute('tabindex', '0');
    fab.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Open cart');
    fab.innerHTML =
      '<span class="pg-cs-msg"></span>' +
      '<span class="pg-cs-ic">' +
        '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">' +
          '<path d="M6 7h12l1 13H5L6 7z"></path><path d="M9 7a3 3 0 0 1 6 0"></path>' +
        '</svg><span class="pg-cs-n" hidden>0</span>' +
      '</span>';
    msg = fab.querySelector('.pg-cs-msg');
    badge = fab.querySelector('.pg-cs-n');

    function go(){
      var h = headerCart();
      if (h) h.click();                 /* reuse the theme's own cart opening */
      else window.location.href = '/cart';
    }
    fab.addEventListener('click', go);
    fab.addEventListener('keydown', function(e){
      if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' '){ e.preventDefault(); go(); }
    });
    document.body.appendChild(fab);
    return fab;
  }

  function paint(){
    if (!msg) return;
    /* WHAT THE LINE SAYS. It used to be one thing or the other: with the offer
       window open it showed the clock and the discount and dropped the item
       count entirely, so a shopper with something in the cart never saw that
       from the bar. Both are on it now -- "2 ITEMS" and the discount -- which
       is also why the discount line is the short form here. "ENTIRE ORDER" is
       what pushed it past the width and produced the ellipsis; the drawer still
       carries the full wording where there is room for it. */
    var r = rush();
    var items = count === 1 ? '1 ITEM' : count + ' ITEMS';
    var html;
    if (count > 0){
      html = (r ? '<span class="pg-cs-clock">' + r.fmt() + '</span>' : '') +
             '<span class="pg-cs-sub">' + items + ' &middot; EXTRA <b>10% OFF</b></span>';
    } else {
      /* nothing in the cart yet: show the offer anyway, with its own clock.
         TO CART is wrapped so the narrow-phone rule can drop it -- see the
         measurement in the note at the top of the file. */
      var p = peekLeft();
      html = (p > 0 ? '<span class="pg-cs-clock">' + clockMs(p) + '</span>' : '') +
             '<span class="pg-cs-sub">ADD<span class="pg-cs-long"> TO CART</span> FOR EXTRA <b>10% OFF</b></span>';
    }
    /* against what we last wrote, not innerHTML read back: `&middot;`
       parses to a literal character, so the read-back never matched the
       source and paint() rewrote the bar on every one-second tick. */
    if (msg.pgHtml !== html){ msg.pgHtml = html; msg.innerHTML = html; }
    /* GUARDED LIKE THE MESSAGE ABOVE. paint() runs on a one-second interval, and
       writing textContent replaces the text node even when the string has not
       changed -- a childList record on every tick, forever. Three modules watch
       document.body for childList mutations (pg-cart-mobile's apply(), pg-chips'
       stamp(), pg-unlock's scheduleRender()), so an unguarded write here was a
       1 Hz metronome driving a cart-wide re-layout pass and a /cart.js read for
       as long as the page stayed open. */
    if (badge){
      if (count > 0){
        var bt = count > 99 ? '99+' : String(count);
        if (badge.textContent !== bt) badge.textContent = bt;
        if (badge.hidden) badge.hidden = false;
      } else if (!badge.hidden) badge.hidden = true;
    }
  }

  /* WHEN IT COMES IN. It used to be on from first paint, and on a phone that
     put it straight over the POCKET ERA wordmark and the announcement carousel
     at the very top of the page -- it was hiding the two things a shopper looks
     at first. It now waits until the page's own cart control has scrolled
     slightly out of view, which is the exact moment the bar starts being
     useful: before that the header icon is right there, after it there is
     nothing else to tap. The anchor is the header cart link, falling back to
     the header itself and then to a plain 140px of scroll if neither is in the
     DOM yet. GRACE of 6px means "slightly", not "completely" -- the bar arrives
     as the icon clears the top edge rather than a screen later. */
  var GRACE = 6;
  function pastHeader(){
    var a = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-cart') ||
            document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-ic') ||
            document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-head');
    if (!a) return (window.pageYOffset || document.documentElement.scrollTop || 0) > 140;
    return a.getBoundingClientRect().bottom < GRACE;
  }

  function sync(){
    if (!document.getElementById('pgx')) return;
    var f = build();
    /* once there is a cart, pg-cart-timer's window is the real one, so the
       preview deadline is dropped and a later empty cart starts a fresh one */
    if (count > 0) peekClear();
    f.classList.toggle('pg-cs-on', pastHeader());
    paint();
  }

  var busy = false;
  function refresh(){
    if (busy) return;
    busy = true;
    fetch('/cart.js', {headers: {'Accept': 'application/json'}, credentials: 'same-origin'})
      .then(function(r){ return r.ok ? r.json() : null; })
      .then(function(j){
        if (j && typeof j.item_count === 'number') count = j.item_count;
        busy = false; sync();
      })
      .catch(function(){ busy = false; });
  }

  function boot(){
    if (!document.getElementById('pgx')) return;   /* product pages only */
    build();
    refresh();

    window.addEventListener('scroll', sync, {passive: true});
    window.addEventListener('resize', sync);
    setInterval(paint, 1000);                      /* keeps the countdown moving */

    document.addEventListener('click', function(e){
      if (!e.target.closest) return;
      if (e.target.closest('[name="add"], .pgx-atc, #pg-sticky, .pg-unlock-go, form[action*="/cart/add"] button')){
        setTimeout(refresh, 600); setTimeout(refresh, 1800);
      }
    }, true);

    if (window.MutationObserver){
      var drawer = document.getElementById('cart');
      if (drawer){
        var queued = false;
        new MutationObserver(function(){
          if (queued) return;
          queued = true;
          setTimeout(function(){ queued = false; refresh(); }, 250);
        }).observe(drawer, {childList: true, subtree: true});
      }
    }

    [900, 2600].forEach(function(t){ setTimeout(refresh, t); });
  }

  if (document.readyState === 'loading') document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', boot);
  else boot();
})();
</script>
{% schema %}
{"name": "PG Cart Sticky", "settings": [], "presets": [{"name": "PG Cart Sticky"}]}
{% endschema %}
